    "# Final Homework: Unsupervised Learning, Dimensionality Reduction, and Anomaly Detection\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1. Dataset Selection & Context\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1.1 Provenance and Collecting Agency\n",
    "* **Dataset Name:** Kepler Exoplanet Search Results (`cumulative.csv`).\n",
    "* **Source:** NASA Exoplanet Archive (IPAC / Caltech / NASA).\n",
    "* **Collecting Mission:** NASA Kepler Space Observatory.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1.2 Purpose of Collection\n",
    "The Kepler mission was designed to survey a portion of the Milky Way galaxy using high-precision transit photometry to determine the frequency of Earth-sized planets within or near habitable zones. The telescope monitored over 150,000 main-sequence stars for periodic dips in brightness caused by transiting planets.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1.3 Feature Descriptions\n",
    "The feature space is strictly continuous and numerical, capturing stellar astrophysics and transit geometry:\n",
    "1. `koi_period`: Orbital period of the candidate [days].\n",
    "2. `koi_time0bk`: Transit epoch [BJD - 2454833.0].\n",
    "3. `koi_impact`: Sky-projected impact parameter of the transit chord ($b \\in [0, 1)$ for central transits, $\\ge 1$ for grazing).\n",
    "4. `koi_duration`: Duration of the transit event [hours].\n",
    "5. `koi_depth`: Transit depth [parts per million, ppm], representing the flux reduction $(\\Delta F / F \\approx (R_p / R_*)^2)$.\n",
    "6. `koi_prad`: Planetary radius [Earth radii, $R_\\oplus$].\n",
    "7. `koi_teq`: Approximate equilibrium temperature of the planet [Kelvin].\n",
    "8. `koi_insol`: Insolation flux received by the candidate relative to Earth [$S_\\oplus$].\n",
    "9. `koi_steff`: Stellar effective surface temperature [Kelvin].\n",
    "10. `koi_slogg`: Surface gravity of the host star [$\\log_{10}(\\text{cm/s}^2)$].\n",
    "11. `koi_srad`: Photospheric radius of the host star [Solar radii, $R_\\odot$].\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1.4 Data Limitations, Systematic Biases, and Artifacts\n",
    "* **Geometric Transit Probability:** Planets with wider orbits have dramatically lower transit probabilities ($P_{tr} \\propto R_* / a$), creating a severe observational selection bias toward short-period bodies.\n",
    "* **Sensitivity Thresholds:** Small rocky planets orbiting faint stars fall below detection thresholds, skewing observable distributions.\n",
    "* **Instrumental Noise & Artifacts:** Telescope pointing jitter, thermal breathing of the spacecraft, and rolling band noise introduce spurious signals resembling astrophysical transits."
   ]
  },
