    "## 3. Dimensionality Reduction: Principal Component Analysis (PCA)\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3.1 Mathematical Foundation\n",
    "Principal Component Analysis (PCA) performs an orthogonal linear transformation that maps the data to a new coordinate system such that the greatest variance by any projection lies on the first coordinate (first principal component), the second greatest variance on the second, and so on. Given a standardized data matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times d}$, PCA solves the eigenvalue problem for the sample covariance matrix $\\mathbf{\\Sigma} = \\frac{1}{n}\\mathbf{X}^T \\mathbf{X}$:\n",
    "$$\\mathbf{\\Sigma} \\mathbf{v}_i = \\lambda_i \\mathbf{v}_i$$\n",
    "where $\\lambda_1 \\ge \\lambda_2 \\ge \\dots \\ge \\lambda_d \\ge 0$ are the eigenvalues (representing the variance along each component) and $\\mathbf{v}_i$ are the corresponding orthonormal eigenvectors (loadings).\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3.2 Discussion and Critical Reflection\n",
    "* **Variance Retention & Dimensionality Loss:**\n",
    "  The scree plot and cumulative explained variance ratio indicate how many components are required to capture substantial variance. Approximately 6–7 components are needed to surpass $80\\%$ cumulative explained variance, proving that the original 11-dimensional space cannot be collapsed into 2D without losing critical subtle geometric characteristics.\n",
    "* **Variable Correlations & Component Interpretation:**\n",
    "  Stellar radius (`koi_srad`) and stellar surface gravity (`koi_slogg`) have a strong inverse correlation ($g \\propto M_* / R_*^2$), dominating the primary principal components. The first component primarily represents stellar host scale, while the second reflects transit depth and planetary size.\n",
    "* **Redundancy & Global vs. Local Geometry:**\n",
    "  PCA captures **global linear variance**. It does not model non-linear orbital dynamics (e.g., Kepler's Third Law $T^2 \\propto a^3$) or fine-grained local cluster structures, meaning proximity in 2D PCA space does not guarantee proximity on the underlying non-linear manifold."
   ]
