    "## 4. Clustering Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4.1 Sample Clustering Methods & Mathematical Intuitions\n",
    "Three distinct clustering paradigms are implemented:\n",
    "1. **K-Means (Centroid-based):** Minimizes within-cluster sum of squares (inertia):\n",
    "   $$J = \\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\sum_{x \\in C_k} \\|x - \\mu_k\\|^2$$\n",
    "   *Assumptions:* Spherical clusters, equal cluster variance, linearly separable Voronoi cells.\n",
    "2. **DBSCAN (Density-based):** Identifies high-density regions separated by low-density zones using parameters $\\epsilon$ (neighborhood radius) and $MinPts$. It natively segregates noise points without forcing every sample into a cluster.\n",
    "   *Assumptions:* Clusters are continuous dense manifolds of arbitrary shape.\n",
    "3. **Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering (Connectivity-based):** Builds a bottom-up tree by recursively merging clusters that minimize the Ward variance criterion.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4.2 Clustering on the Feature Space (Transposed Data Matrix)\n",
    "Applying clustering to the transpose of the data matrix ($\\mathbf{X}^T \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times n}$) clusters the variables based on their cross-sample activation profiles:\n",
    "* **Redundant Feature Groups:** Stellar properties (`koi_slogg`, `koi_srad`, `koi_steff`) cluster together tightly, revealing physical co-dependence.\n",
    "* **Isolated Features:** `koi_depth` and `koi_impact` exhibit distinct orthogonal profiles, indicating that they introduce independent geometric information not duplicated by stellar features.\n",
    "* **Domain Utility:** Feature clustering provides automated feature selection, collinearity diagnosis, and discovery of latent physical mechanisms.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4.3 Evaluation Metric: Within-Cluster Variance vs. Global Variance\n",
    "The assignment asks to evaluate the ratio of average within-cluster variance to global variance:\n",
    "$$\\text{Variance Ratio} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\text{Var}(C_k)}{\\text{Var}_{\\text{global}}}$$\n",
    "* *Is this metric suitable?* It is suitable for centroid-based models (like K-Means) that explicitly optimize variance. However, it penalizes non-convex or elongated clusters (like those found by DBSCAN) and does not penalize overlapping clusters. The **Silhouette Coefficient** serves as an indispensable complementary metric that balances cohesion with separation."
   ]
  },
