    "## 5. Multi-Dimensional Anomaly Detection\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5.1 Z-Score Method\n",
    "* **Mathematical Intuition:** Measures deviation from the univariate mean in units of standard deviation: $Z_{ij} = \\frac{x_{ij} - \\mu_j}{\\sigma_j}$. An observation is flagged if its maximum absolute Z-score across features exceeds a threshold (e.g., $Z_{\\text{threshold}} = 3.0$):\n",
    "  $$Z_{\\text{global}}(x_i) = \\max_j |Z_{ij}|$$\n",
    "* **Limitations in High Dimensions:** Assumes univariate normality. It fails to account for inter-feature covariance and fails completely on non-convex or correlated anomalies (e.g., a data point with normal values on all individual features that represents an impossible physical combination).\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5.2 Isolation Forest\n",
    "* **Mechanism:** Exploits the fact that anomalies are \"few and different\". The algorithm builds an ensemble of completely random trees that partition feature values with random orthogonal cuts. Because anomalies reside in sparse, peripheral regions, they are isolated near the root with substantially shorter average path lengths $h(x)$.\n",
    "* **Anomaly Score:** \n",
    "  $$s(x, n) = 2^{-\\frac{\\mathbb{E}(h(x))}{c(n)}}$$\n",
    "  where $c(n)$ is the average path length of unsuccessful searches in a Binary Search Tree. An anomaly score close to 1 indicates an anomaly, while scores well below 0.5 indicate normal observations.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5.3 Local Outlier Factor (LOF)\n",
    "* **Local Density Estimation:** Compares the local density of an observation to that of its $k$-nearest neighbors. An observation with a significantly lower local density than its neighbors is identified as an outlier:\n",
    "  $$\\text{LOF}_k(p) = \\frac{\\sum_{o \\in N_k(p)} \\frac{\\text{lrd}(o)}{\\text{lrd}(p)}}{|N_k(p)|}$$\n",
    "* **Global vs. Local Anomalies:** Unlike Z-scores (which identify only global outliers at the extreme edges), LOF detects subtle, local anomalies embedded near varying-density clusters."
   ]
