    "## 6. Anomaly Detection Methods: Comprehensive Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6.1 Method Agreement & Divergence\n",
    "* **Consensus Anomalies:** Observations flagged by all three algorithms represent massive, extreme physical outliers (e.g., ultra-deep eclipses with $R_p > 50 R_\\oplus$ or extreme stellar temperatures $T_{\\text{eff}} > 10,000\\text{ K}$).\n",
    "* **Method-Specific Anomalies:**\n",
    "  * *Z-Score:* Identifies exclusively extreme coordinate values along single feature axes, ignoring multivariate correlations.\n",
    "  * *Isolation Forest:* Excels at detecting global subspace anomalies formed by rare combinations of physical attributes.\n",
    "  * *LOF:* Detects isolated candidates in locally dense regions, even if their individual coordinate values appear normal globally.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6.2 Sensitivity to Dimensionality, Scaling, and Runtime\n",
    "* **Feature Scaling:** Z-Score and LOF rely directly on metric distances and are highly sensitive to feature scaling. Isolation Forest uses orthogonal binary partitions and is invariant to monotonic feature scaling.\n",
    "* **Curse of Dimensionality:** LOF scales poorly in high dimensions due to metric space decay, whereas Isolation Forest maintains robust performance by evaluating random subsets of features.\n",
    "* **Computational Cost:** Z-Score is $O(n \\cdot d)$ (instantaneous). Isolation Forest is $O(t \\cdot n \\log n)$ (fast and parallel). LOF is $O(n^2 \\cdot d)$ (slow on large datasets).\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6.3 Real-World False Positives vs. False Negatives (Non-Technical Explanation)\n",
    "* **False Positive (False Alarm):** Flagging a rare, legitimate exoplanet (e.g., an Earth-sized planet orbiting in a cold, wide habitable zone) as an abnormal anomaly or bad data. \n",
    "  * *Consequence:* High-value scientific targets are discarded automatically before astronomical confirmation.\n",
    "* **False Negative (Missed Anomaly):** Failing to identify an eclipsing binary star or spacecraft instrumental glitch, categorizing it as a normal planet candidate.\n",
    "  * *Consequence:* Ground-based observatories waste high-value telescope time attempting to confirm an astronomical false alarm."
   ]
